WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
We want to divide h of one, P, h
of negative one, P, and h of i,

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:10.140
P by two, while keeping integer
or Gaussian integer coefficients.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.760
It is enough to check one
power, P to the b, at a time.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.640
Select that same position in
the four sign polynomials.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.670
Call its coefficients a zero,
a one, a two, and a three.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:26.620
Each is positive or negative
one, so each is odd.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:32.120
Keep the power P to the b fixed while
we calculate with its four coefficients.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:38.510
At Z equal to one, the coefficient
is the sum of all four signs.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.840
At Z equal to negative one, the signs
on a one and a three are reversed.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.800
Work modulo two: both positive and
negative versions of an odd integer

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.980
have residue one. Each expression
therefore becomes four ones.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.470
Pair the ones: each pair has even
sum, and the total has residue zero.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.300
Return to the original
integer expressions.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.600
Both are even, so dividing
either by two gives an integer.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.920
We have justified the two
real half coefficients.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:15.140
At Z equal to i, separate the
coefficient into a zero minus a two,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.360
plus i times the difference
of a one and a three.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.780
Each difference of two signs is even.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.130
Divide the real difference and the
imaginary difference separately by two.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.040
Both halves are integers, so their
combination is a Gaussian integer.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.330
For one coefficient check, choose
the column negative one, one,

00:01:35.765 --> 00:01:39.220
one, negative one. The
first real half is zero.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.540
The second real half is also zero. The
Gaussian half is negative one plus i.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.940
This is one column illustrating
parity, not a constructed Hadamard row.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:53.440
Repeat the same argument
independently at every power of P.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.840
Therefore the first two
polynomials have integer

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.780
half coefficients, and the third has
Gaussian integer half coefficients.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.980
The proof uses only the common
signs. No norm identity is needed.
